  <notes>Mandate: owner round 9 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md). Evidence: notes/route-authorization-sweep-2026-10-02.md; the full traces are in the sweep's idor-sweep.json (a session scratch file; the path is in relevant-files).

Fix-task mapping, as given by the main session:
- 159 events (GitHub #1098);
- 160 Api/Dataverse;
- 161 communications;
- 162 AnalysisEndpoints;
- 163 RAG/knowledge/insights/workspace-ai. AdminKnowledgeEndpoints is assigned here as "knowledge", even though its routes are under /api/admin.
- 164 chat/agent/playbooks/prompts/record-match;
- 165 SpeAdmin + Admin/RecordMatching;
- 166 the rest.

This task changes no runtime behaviour, so the caller-level negative case belongs to each fix task: a caller with no rights on the target gets the fail-closed answer, and an unknown id gets the same answer. Here, the negative case for every route is the guard's own: with the route's waiver removed, the build fails naming the route.

The scope is widened from Api/** to every census file. The census already counts the three files outside Api/**, and round 9 says "every BFF route". Leaving them ungoverned would leave a route surface that the census sees and the rule ignores, which is why GET /healthz/dataverse/doc/{id} was never seen.

The six PermanentBasis values read the owner's round-9 waiver class ("a route that only touches the caller's own data") as covering routes that touch no other principal's record: anonymous probes, reference data, creates that name nothing, caller-supplied content, and an operator gate in the handler. The main session should confirm that reading with the owner. Until then, escalation trigger 2 stops any further widening.

The route counts in the background come from a Python approximation of the scanner run at authoring. They are guidance, not acceptance values.</notes>

